---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { SITE } from "@/data/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Disclaimer"
  description="TinyTools calculators produce estimates, not official figures. How overtime presets, pay conversions, and 2026 paycheck withholding estimates are calculated, and their limits."
  path="/disclaimer/"
>
  <div class="container prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Disclaimer" }]} />
    <h1>Disclaimer</h1>
    <p>Last updated: <time datetime="2026-08-31">31 August 2026</time></p>

    <p>
      Every result on {SITE.name} is an <strong>estimate</strong> generated from
      the inputs you enter and from published rates and formulas. It is not an
      official calculation, a payslip, or a tax determination.
    </p>

    <h2>Hours and time calculations</h2>
    <p>
      Time and time-card tools use standard arithmetic: elapsed time between a
      start and end, minus unpaid breaks, with shifts that pass midnight counted
      into the next day. Rounding (if you enable it) follows the increment you
      choose. These figures are only as accurate as the times you enter.
    </p>

    <h2>Overtime</h2>
    <p>
      The overtime rule presets (US FLSA 40-hour week, California daily overtime,
      Ontario 44-hour week, the UK, and a common Australian award pattern) are
      <strong>calculation conveniences, not legal advice</strong>. Whether you are
      entitled to overtime, and at what rate, depends on your employee
      classification, your award or contract, your state or province, and current
      law. The tool applies the thresholds and multipliers you select; it does
      not decide your entitlement. Where a preset omits a rule (for example
      California's seventh-consecutive-day rule), that is stated on the page.
    </p>

    <h2>Salary and pay conversions</h2>
    <p>
      Conversions between hourly, weekly, biweekly, semi-monthly, monthly, and
      annual pay use the hours-per-week and weeks-per-year assumptions shown on
      the page (default: 40 hours, 52 weeks, i.e. 2,080 hours a year). Change
      those to match your real schedule. "Real hourly rate" divides annual pay by
      the hours you actually work after paid time off.
    </p>

    <h2>Paycheck and take-home pay</h2>
    <p>
      The paycheck and take-home tools estimate <strong>payroll withholding for
      United States tax year 2026 only</strong>. They combine:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Federal income tax withholding using the IRS Publication 15-T percentage method (assuming a 2020-or-later Form W-4);</li>
      <li>Social Security (6.2% up to the 2026 wage base of $184,500) and Medicare (1.45%, plus 0.9% Additional Medicare on wages over $200,000);</li>
      <li>State income tax withholding for the supported states only: California, New York, Illinois, Pennsylvania, and the states with no wage income tax (Texas, Florida, Washington, and others).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These are <strong>withholding estimates, not the tax you ultimately owe</strong>.
      Your annual tax return reconciles the difference. The tools do <em>not</em>
      model: tax-return credits and deductions, local city or county taxes (such
      as New York City, Yonkers, or the Philadelphia wage tax), state disability
      or unemployment contributions beyond noted cases, wage garnishments, or
      year-to-date limits across multiple jobs.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you select a state, country, or tax year that is not supported, the tool
      says so clearly and does not invent a number. Canada, the United Kingdom,
      and Australia are not yet supported for paycheck calculations.
    </p>

    <h2>No affiliation with tax authorities</h2>
    <p>
      {SITE.name} is independent and is not connected to any government or revenue
      agency. For an authoritative figure, use your employer's payroll system or
      the official calculators published by the relevant tax authority.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
